here $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [:db/cas 17592186446376 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186446379 :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [:db/cas 17592186446379 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{rank}(A).$" "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{rank}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125757 :tutorial/content "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n![](images/ad/addf5cd1e1219a1e1ce28f3d27d1b000b3a99640ff89a62e55b66f3a5cdb3521.svg)\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/96/96dcf8cf9f0d387e0539ea04dd7ae4a498534361ad6bd5ed3bb119ae21f0416a.svg)\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column."] [:db/cas 17592186124290 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its pivot columns?\n![](images/ce/ce4c17b3e47f0618d315e8e61feeb2b58cd913e54b63a3a375b8bd4676efbc8e.svg)" "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its pivot columns?\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186124290 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n![](images/31/31b76b7840d750cf7cf060c5d218644f8733d46a0b59c0e347aeb634ea6c1091.svg)" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186566216 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186566216 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186566219 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 4 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 & 4 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186566219 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 4 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 & 4 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124291 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $C$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n![](images/b1/b1f927457c74f57ff2e7cdb9d030492fffcb49bdfd9199e8720d3da2263e0b1e.svg)" "The row echelon form matrix $C$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186124291 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n![](images/05/05264585d59a4430f5424b91f649eecc80a5f28fa460a935f5c224882e08d56c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column." "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column."] [:db/cas 17592186566245 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186566245 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column."] [:db/cas 17592186124292 :question/problem "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/df/dfe650778e528254fc2098714566d954c47442ede485d6a92495cf16eb9e936d.svg)" "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186124885 :question/problem "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124885 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(\\frac{2}{7}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $D$ has $5$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $5.$" "First, we need to convert $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(\\frac{2}{7}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $D$ has $5$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124293 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n![](images/f1/f10a5ef881920312ccdb163d9db70779d3848f7dc510c29d516eaf74a5ddd38d.svg)" "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186163222 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/0f/0f62a89f8da1ab7bfb86d46fddb441e95e5e3a9554e5a88662691b4df40e19af.svg)\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n![](images/78/780de3c3f66b1037e4152224f4aa98b65b8b1b859adfe7c3881bb830d7eecd66.svg)\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}4}} \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}2}} & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$"] [:db/cas 17592186161771 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](images/fb/fb428c39bbc03be798bca2463658676de5c684396001cf547e9395352f456bc9.svg)" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186161771 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](images/3f/3f91ec40e717eac93bb38f9c13f7b1700e84ac109ccceaffbb8473ba8d6b4e34.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & {\\color{red}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2}} & -1 \\\\ {\\color{red}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{0}} & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186163254 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n![](images/e4/e41d28b6c6562ada530024fe6ab29cc16447dce14b2f741b8b35758618c78103.svg)\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons."] [:db/cas 17592186161816 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/81/812e9888b920e0338fd72116464b8b96c8c272f95f180cd46aa3f1db9afac8d2.svg)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186121058 :tutorial/content "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n![](images/b9/b91bd53b8f1807be516e2477b37fbcd9c3a7415d6cf848d84aa085bc510b5eb4.svg)\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$" "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186119662 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n![](images/2c/2cd43e61d4c51ee86d78217688daea5f8ebdfa656a9628e4bef3ef4ac9f8bd3a.svg)" "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186119663 :question/problem "If ![](images/82/823965b7b8f818ec843aefc4c4cc16ef2922e269db8d53f37cf633ff132e5783.svg), find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" "If \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n, find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186115056 :question/problem "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n![](images/b4/b4b5ccc4e5075ba81dc840743f0267ba2e5f28d75a5be09de54df111203b949a.svg)" "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} & -45 \\\\ 81 & -\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186235392 :question/problem "$$\nX+Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Knowing that the matrix representation $T=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}_{11} & {t}_{12} \\\\ {t}_{21} & {t}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ of a linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=XZ+YZ,\n$$\n\nfind the values ${t}_{ij}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${t}_{11}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,{t}_{12}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${t}_{21}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,{t}_{22}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-4}}" "$$\nX+Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Knowing that the matrix representation $T=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}_{11} & {t}_{12} \\\\ {t}_{21} & {t}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ of a linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=XZ+YZ,\n$$\n\nfind the values ${t}_{ij}.$\n\n${t}_{11}=$ {{field-1}}\n\n${t}_{12}=$ {{field-2}}\n\n${t}_{21}=$ {{field-3}}\n\n${t}_{22}=$ {{field-4}}"] [:db/cas 17592186115057 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n![](images/32/32c0a3389207452c52376ce4305109e1c5ed634a07d2c99771825c39b5482940.svg)" "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186115838 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC) &=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [:db/cas 17592186345115 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC) &=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [:db/cas 17592186193941 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/72/724119f2404af61332fcabbb5cda3522f9d338a4c6427084e5901e1ca7cd4498.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This will eliminate the variable $x,$ allowing us to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$" "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\phantom{\\,(}x+2y &=1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}(\\,}x-y &=-2{\\color{blue}\\,)}{\\color{blue}\\:\\times (-1)}\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}-x+y} &{\\color{blue}{}=2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This will eliminate the variable $x,$ allowing us to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186192585 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n![](images/2b/2b0aa0d1dbfeb1e711bde6e7741fe5706aa126d71c44ae417d3c78d01b83547a.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-2a}-8b & =-2 \\\\ \\cancel{2a}+b & =-5 \\\\ (-8b+b) & =(-2-5) \\\\ -7b & =-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2},$ and add the equations*." "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}(\\,}a+4b &=1{\\color{blue}\\,)}{\\color{blue}\\:\\times (-2)} \\\\ 2a+b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}-2a-8b} &{\\color{blue}{}=-2} \\\\ 2a+b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-2a}-8b & =-2 \\\\ \\cancel{2a}+b & =-5 \\\\ (-8b+b) & =(-2-5) \\\\ -7b & =-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2},$ and add the equations*."] [:db/cas 17592186193241 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n${x - y = 1; - 7x + 5y = 9$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - y &=1 \\\\ - 7x + 5y &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations."] [:db/cas 17592186192586 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2},$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e494f68d4859d46c3c369274e415f10a850c664343d41cce8dcfcfdd660e17c.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =2 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{4y} & =8 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(2+8) \\\\ 5x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$ and add the equations*." "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2},$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\phantom{\\,(}3x+4y &=2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}(}\\,x-2y &=4\\,{\\color{blue})}\\:{\\color{blue}\\times (2)}\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}2x-4y} &{\\color{blue}{}=8}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =2 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{4y} & =8 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(2+8) \\\\ 5x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$ and add the equations*."] [:db/cas 17592186193243 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n${9x + 10y = 3; - 2x - y = 3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x + 10y &=3 \\\\ - 2x - y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations."] [:db/cas 17592186193244 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n${3w + 2z =-1; 7w + 10z = 3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $z$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3w + 2z &=-1 \\\\ 7w + 10z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $z$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations."] [:db/cas 17592186192587 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n![](images/a7/a7e5f9f09d67dd14a4797e5675585973e3aafeb1ee96e9021d245f49ce9c1c7e.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+3y & =8 \\\\ \\cancel{-2x}+4y & =6 \\\\ (3y+4y) & =(8+6) \\\\ 7y & =14 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =-3 \\\\ x-2(2) & =-3 \\\\ x-4 & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(1,2).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\phantom{\\,(}2x+3y &=8 \\\\ (\\,x-2y &=-3\\,)\\:\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=8 \\\\ -2x+4y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+3y & =8 \\\\ \\cancel{-2x}+4y & =6 \\\\ (3y+4y) & =(8+6) \\\\ 7y & =14 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =-3 \\\\ x-2(2) & =-3 \\\\ x-4 & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(1,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186193236 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n${4x + 3y = 5; - x + 7y =-9$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + 3y &=5 \\\\ - x + 7y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$."] [:db/cas 17592186192588 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n![](images/e7/e701d299976539abdbbe6e9c311c2db14c23cca6817c5a3de5b5c05a95760d99.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5u-\\cancel{6v} & =3 \\\\ -9u+\\cancel{6v} & =-15 \\\\ (5u-9u) & =(3-15) \\\\ -4u & =-12 \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+2v & =-5 \\\\ -3(3)+2v & =-5 \\\\ -9+2v & =-5 \\\\ 2v & =4 \\\\ v & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u = 3, \\,v = 2,$ which can be expressed as $(3,2).$" "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v &=3 \\\\ (\\,-3u+2v &=-5\\,)\\:\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v &=3 \\\\ -9u+6v &=-15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5u-\\cancel{6v} & =3 \\\\ -9u+\\cancel{6v} & =-15 \\\\ (5u-9u) & =(3-15) \\\\ -4u & =-12 \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+2v & =-5 \\\\ -3(3)+2v & =-5 \\\\ -9+2v & =-5 \\\\ 2v & =4 \\\\ v & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u = 3, \\,v = 2,$ which can be expressed as $(3,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186193212 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${3x - 2y =-1; 4x + y =-5$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ 4x + y &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186075061 :tutorial/content "The systems of equations we've encountered up to now have all had *exactly one solution*.\n\nFor example, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 2x + 1; y = 1 - x\n$$\n\nUsing either substitution or elimination, we can show that the solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $(0,1)$. This is the *only* solution, and it tells us the point of intersection between the two corresponding lines.\n\n![](images/bd/bd7edb530e16b853318a3e38785c7b6353c1b54f10ee3c4e2de7da915450f6d9.png)\n\nIn general, systems of equations can have\n\n- *exactly one* solution (like the example above)\n- *infinitely many* solutions\n- *no solutions*\n\nTo determine the number of solutions to a system of equations, we solve it using either substitution or elimination and interpret the result.\n\nLet's look at a system of equations with infinitely many solutions." "The systems of equations we've encountered up to now have all had *exactly one solution*.\n\nFor example, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x + 1 \\\\ y &=1 - x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUsing either substitution or elimination, we can show that the solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $(0,1)$. This is the *only* solution, and it tells us the point of intersection between the two corresponding lines.\n\n![](images/bd/bd7edb530e16b853318a3e38785c7b6353c1b54f10ee3c4e2de7da915450f6d9.png)\n\nIn general, systems of equations can have\n\n- *exactly one* solution (like the example above)\n- *infinitely many* solutions\n- *no solutions*\n\nTo determine the number of solutions to a system of equations, we solve it using either substitution or elimination and interpret the result.\n\nLet's look at a system of equations with infinitely many solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186075063 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2; 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nTo show that this system has infinitely many solutions, we solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n6x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always* true. This implies that this system of equations has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3x - y =-2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6x -2y=-4}\n$$\n\nboth represent the *same straight line*. In other words, the two lines **coincide**.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e98008aa5972889efdd3af0689da310c69bca06dd410a391a37e22aa8b0098.png)\n\nThese two lines intersect at an infinite number of points. The system has infinitely many solutions because there are infinitely many intersection points.\n\nAnother way to see that this system has infinitely many solutions is to notice that the lines are nonzero multiples of each other.\n\nFor example, if we multiply the equation of the first line by $2$, we get the second line!\n\n$$\n3x - y =-2; 2 \\cdot (3x - y) = 2 \\cdot (-2); 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a system with *no solutions*." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo show that this system has infinitely many solutions, we solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=-2 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n6x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always* true. This implies that this system of equations has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3x - y =-2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6x -2y=-4}\n$$\n\nboth represent the *same straight line*. In other words, the two lines **coincide**.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e98008aa5972889efdd3af0689da310c69bca06dd410a391a37e22aa8b0098.png)\n\nThese two lines intersect at an infinite number of points. The system has infinitely many solutions because there are infinitely many intersection points.\n\nAnother way to see that this system has infinitely many solutions is to notice that the lines are nonzero multiples of each other.\n\nFor example, if we multiply the equation of the first line by $2$, we get the second line!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=-2 \\\\ 2\\cdot(3x-y) &=2\\cdot(-2) \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a system with *no solutions*."] [:db/cas 17592186073480 :question/problem "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n${4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4$" "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + 2y &=4 \\\\ 2x + y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073480 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)" "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=4 \\\\ 2x+y &=4 && \\text{multiply by } -2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186074059 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n${6x + 4y = 6; - 2x - y = 5$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 4y &=6 \\\\ - 2x - y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186074086 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n${y - 3x - 2 = 0; 4y - 12x - 8 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y - 3x - 2 &=0 \\\\ 4y - 12x - 8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186074087 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n${2x - 5y = 15; 4x - 10y =-10$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 5y &=15 \\\\ 4x - 10y &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186075049 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the solutions of a linear system\n\n$$\n{a_{1}x + b_{1}y = c_{1}; a_{2}x + b_{2}y = c_{2}\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the points of intersection of the two lines. There are three possibilities:\n\n| ![](images/a1/a1e58afecd7b919f6fd77b48f7bcb7219e54d2f86f8ef07df619505a4643bdd4.png) | ![](images/a4/a466524eb95d85e6c383a37955fffbf02fc9391b0b09a85fce7f44ab934bdc91.png) | ![](images/c7/c73a9f1bfbdd4efa1826b77a7bce1a9e1cbb13e61d718ffe18404e13a31a34f4.png) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n- Case $(a)$ shows two parallel lines. Because there are no points of intersection, the system has *no solutions*. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope but *different* $y$-intercepts.\n- Case $(b)$ shows two lines intersecting at a single point. Because there is a single point of intersection, the system has *one solution*.\n- Case $(c)$ shows two lines that coincide. Because there are infinitely many points of intersection, the system has *infinitely many* solutions. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope and *same* $y$-intercept. In such cases, the two lines are nonzero multiples of each other." "As we've seen, the solutions of a linear system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a_{1}x + b_{1}y &=c_{1} \\\\ a_{2}x + b_{2}y &=c_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the points of intersection of the two lines. There are three possibilities:\n\n| ![](images/a1/a1e58afecd7b919f6fd77b48f7bcb7219e54d2f86f8ef07df619505a4643bdd4.png) | ![](images/a4/a466524eb95d85e6c383a37955fffbf02fc9391b0b09a85fce7f44ab934bdc91.png) | ![](images/c7/c73a9f1bfbdd4efa1826b77a7bce1a9e1cbb13e61d718ffe18404e13a31a34f4.png) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n- Case $(a)$ shows two parallel lines. Because there are no points of intersection, the system has *no solutions*. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope but *different* $y$-intercepts.\n- Case $(b)$ shows two lines intersecting at a single point. Because there is a single point of intersection, the system has *one solution*.\n- Case $(c)$ shows two lines that coincide. Because there are infinitely many points of intersection, the system has *infinitely many* solutions. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope and *same* $y$-intercept. In such cases, the two lines are nonzero multiples of each other."] [:db/cas 17592186073481 :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 2; 10y - 5x =-10\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)." "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=2 \\\\ 10y - 5x &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."] [:db/cas 17592186075062 :tutorial/content "When a system of equations has one solution, we can describe the solution as a single point $(x,y)$. This represents the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nNow, suppose a system of equations has *infinitely many* solutions. Can we describe the solutions to the system in a similar way?\n\nIndeed, we can. To demonstrate, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2; 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that this system has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the corresponding lines coincide.\n\nOur goal is to express *all* the solutions of this system in the form $({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}})$. To do this, we find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the equations for $y$. It doesn't matter which, so let's pick the first equation.\n\n$$\n3x - y =-2⇒y = 3x + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any given $x$-value, the corresponding $y$-value is\n\n$$\ny = 3x+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the intersection points as\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}}) = \\left({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{3x+2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x$ is *any* number." "When a system of equations has one solution, we can describe the solution as a single point $(x,y)$. This represents the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nNow, suppose a system of equations has *infinitely many* solutions. Can we describe the solutions to the system in a similar way?\n\nIndeed, we can. To demonstrate, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that this system has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the corresponding lines coincide.\n\nOur goal is to express *all* the solutions of this system in the form $({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}})$. To do this, we find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the equations for $y$. It doesn't matter which, so let's pick the first equation.\n\n$$\n3x - y =-2⇒y = 3x + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any given $x$-value, the corresponding $y$-value is\n\n$$\ny = 3x+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the intersection points as\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}}) = \\left({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{3x+2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x$ is *any* number."] [:db/cas 17592186075064 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our system of equations:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2; 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nWe showed that the set of intersection points $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) = \\left(x,3x + 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number.\n\nThis formula allows us to generate as many intersection points as we want! To do this, we select *any* $x$-value and substitute it into the formula above.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we set $x=0$, we get the point\n$(x, y)|= (0, 3(0) + 2); = (0, 2)$.\nThis point is indeed a solution to the system:\n${3(0) - 2 =-2; 6(0) - 2(2) =-4$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n$(x, y)|= (1, 3(1) + 2); = (1, 5)$.\nThis point is another solution to the system:\n${3(1) - 5 =-2; 6(1) - 2(5) =-4$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines." "Let's go back to our system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe showed that the set of intersection points $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) = \\left(x,3x + 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number.\n\nThis formula allows us to generate as many intersection points as we want! To do this, we select *any* $x$-value and substitute it into the formula above.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we set $x=0$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (0, 3(0) + 2) = (0, 2)$.\nThis point is indeed a solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(0) - 2 &=-2 \\\\ 6(0) - 2(2) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (1, 3(1) + 2) = (1, 5)$.\nThis point is another solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(1) - 5 &=-2 \\\\ 6(1) - 2(5) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines."] [:db/cas 17592186073482 :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2; 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number." "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + 2y &=2 \\\\ 6x + 4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=2 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 6x+4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186100694 :question/problem "Find $CD$, if \n![](images/1a/1a753977affc5cc3dda18c9206116e1132254bccebd1e3f2545bea75bcc38f18.png)\nand \n![](images/b9/b99016bca94adc5de11eddef950fa13fc4349aaf95387468d07f294c409d029d.png)" "Find $CD$, if \n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nand \n\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186100694 :question/worked-solution "Here, $C$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D,$ so $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{C} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{D} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{CD}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $CD,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=1\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,4=2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/64/64b831a66cdd9a3176d1eb916dfae189b41eb1f0f3ed3184797f92e404957878.png)" "Here, $C$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D,$ so $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{C} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{D} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{CD}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $CD,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=1\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,4=2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nCD=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186100695 :question/problem "Let $C=AB.$ What is the value of $c_{32}$, if\n![](images/c6/c6c7e580cfd96bc23c55742535ceb40a94635c2a44b911e0d08311c3a599c2fa.png)" "Let $C=AB.$ What is the value of $c_{32}$, if\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186100741 :question/problem "Compute the product \n $\\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n       -1  \\\\\n \t0  \\\\\n        -2\n \t\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack.$" "Compute the product \n $\\begin{bmatrix}\n       -1  \\\\\n \t0  \\\\\n        -2\n \t\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186100741 :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \n\t\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n       -1  \\\\\n \t0  \\\\\n        -2\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        (-1)\\cdot 2 & (-1)\\cdot (-5)  \\\\\n        0\\cdot 2 & 0\\cdot (-5) \\\\\n        (-2)\\cdot 2 & (-2)\\cdot (-5)\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack \\[5pt]\n\n\t&=\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n       -2 & 5  \\\\\n       0 &  0  \\\\\n       -4 &  10 \n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack \n}\n$$" "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \n\t\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n       -1  \\\\\n \t0  \\\\\n        -2\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n\t&= \n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        (-1)\\cdot 2 & (-1)\\cdot (-5)  \\\\\n        0\\cdot 2 & 0\\cdot (-5) \\\\\n        (-2)\\cdot 2 & (-2)\\cdot (-5)\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n\n\t&=\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n       -2 & 5  \\\\\n       0 &  0  \\\\\n       -4 &  10 \n    \\end{bmatrix} \n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186116488 :tutorial/content "Powers of matrices are similar to powers of numbers. If $A$ is a matrix, then $A^n$ is $A$ multiplied by itself $n$ times: \n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\:\\cdot\\: A\\:\\cdot\\: \\ldots \\:\\cdot\\: A}_{\\Large{n\\text{ times}}}\n$$\n\nFor $A$ to be conformable with itself, $A$ needs to be a square matrix. So we can only take powers of square matrices.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that\n![](images/81/81537bed0eafc982114b89fc84baeac44f7a75997c89df5a0f89b8b0a00b2da3.svg)\n\nThen we can calculate $A^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that $A^0 = I,$ the identity matrix of the same dimension as $A.$" "Powers of matrices are similar to powers of numbers. If $A$ is a matrix, then $A^n$ is $A$ multiplied by itself $n$ times: \n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\:\\cdot\\: A\\:\\cdot\\: \\ldots \\:\\cdot\\: A}_{\\Large{n\\text{ times}}}\n$$\n\nFor $A$ to be conformable with itself, $A$ needs to be a square matrix. So we can only take powers of square matrices.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nThen we can calculate $A^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that $A^0 = I,$ the identity matrix of the same dimension as $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115059 :question/problem "Find $A^4$ given that ![](images/e0/e0e7d5ed9b893352d68931ff5d8fa93f0d0c5173e5955a3f5cdc0cb65d3c3c78.svg)" "Find $A^4$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186116489 :tutorial/content "Now that we know how to compute powers of matrices, we can evaluate polynomials where the input is a square matrix. \n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3.\n$$\n\nNote that, in order to evaluate this polynomial for an input matrix $X,$ we need to interpret any constants as multiples of the identity matrix. So, we interpret the polynomial as\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3I.\n$$\n\nFor example, if ![](images/6a/6abb7cbdef9c5d7b23d8519468b174de369cfe4af4b9f8c4578ce7dc74812f63.svg) then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can evaluate $f(A)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+4A+3I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+4+3 & 3+4+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 & 4+8+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Now that we know how to compute powers of matrices, we can evaluate polynomials where the input is a square matrix. \n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3.\n$$\n\nNote that, in order to evaluate this polynomial for an input matrix $X,$ we need to interpret any constants as multiples of the identity matrix. So, we interpret the polynomial as\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3I.\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can evaluate $f(A)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+4A+3I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+4+3 & 3+4+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 & 4+8+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115060 :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n![](images/f5/f5b9dd39e03e7a8c984d6a38db8dc32326669d134ff0b5aa012c421308b48351.svg)\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$" "Consider the matrix\n\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} .\n$$\n\n\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186060928 :tutorial/content "To take the **transpose of a matrix**, we interchange each of its columns with the corresponding row. That is, row $1$ becomes column $1,$ row $2$ becomes column $2,$ and so on. A superscript ${}^{T}$ is used to denote the transpose operation.\n\nFor example, the transpose of the $2 \\times 3$ matrix ${X}$ is the following $3 \\times 2$ matrix $X^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{X} = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t{\\color{red}7} & {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}-4}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{blue}-3} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}0}\n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n\n        \\qquad\\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{X}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t{\\color{red}7} & {\\color{blue}-3}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{blue}1}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{red}-4} & {\\color{blue}0}\n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nThe first row of $X$ becomes the first column of $X^T,$ and the second row of $X$ becomes the second column of $X^T.$\n\nNote that transposing a matrix swaps its dimensions. In general, we have the following property:\n\n> *When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $m \\times n$ to $n \\times m.$*\n\nAlso, notice that the transpose of a column vector is a row vector:\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t5  \\\\\n        \t\t4  \\\\\n                        2 \n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n\n       \\qquad \\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t5 & 4 & 2\n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the transpose of a row vector is a column vector:\n\n$$\n{B} = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t8 & -10 & 0 & 6\n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n\n        \\qquad\\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{B}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t8  \\\\\n                        -10  \\\\\n                        0  \\\\\n                        6\n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$" "To take the **transpose of a matrix**, we interchange each of its columns with the corresponding row. That is, row $1$ becomes column $1,$ row $2$ becomes column $2,$ and so on. A superscript ${}^{T}$ is used to denote the transpose operation.\n\nFor example, the transpose of the $2 \\times 3$ matrix ${X}$ is the following $3 \\times 2$ matrix $X^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{X} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t{\\color{red}7} & {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}-4}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{blue}-3} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}0}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n        \\qquad\\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{X}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t{\\color{red}7} & {\\color{blue}-3}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{blue}1}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{red}-4} & {\\color{blue}0}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe first row of $X$ becomes the first column of $X^T,$ and the second row of $X$ becomes the second column of $X^T.$\n\nNote that transposing a matrix swaps its dimensions. In general, we have the following property:\n\n> *When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $m \\times n$ to $n \\times m.$*\n\nAlso, notice that the transpose of a column vector is a row vector:\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t5  \\\\\n        \t\t4  \\\\\n                        2 \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n       \\qquad \\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t5 & 4 & 2\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the transpose of a row vector is a column vector:\n\n$$\n{B} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t8 & -10 & 0 & 6\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n        \\qquad\\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{B}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t8  \\\\\n                        -10  \\\\\n                        0  \\\\\n                        6\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186295249 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $C.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${C}^{T}=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $C.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${C}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186295250 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $E.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${E}^{T}=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-7}}</td><td>{{field-8}}</td><td>{{field-9}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $E.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${E}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-7}}</td><td>{{field-8}}</td><td>{{field-9}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186295251 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$G=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $G.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${G}^{T}=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-7}}</td><td>{{field-8}}</td><td>{{field-9}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$G=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $G.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${G}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-7}}</td><td>{{field-8}}</td><td>{{field-9}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186059553 :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns. \n\nHere, there is just a single row, so it becomes a single column:\n\n$$\n{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n    5  \\\\\n    -1  \\\\\n    0  \\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$" "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns. \n\nHere, there is just a single row, so it becomes a single column:\n\n$$\n{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n    5  \\\\\n    -1  \\\\\n    0  \\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292842 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $D.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${D}^{T}=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $D.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${D}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186292843 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}u & -v & -x & y\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $H.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${H}^{T}=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}u & -v & -x & y\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $H.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${H}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186295255 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $Z.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${Z}^{T}=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $Z.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${Z}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186060929 :tutorial/content "Remember that transposing a matrix swaps the rows and columns. So, if $B=A^T,$ then $b_{ij}=a_{ji}$ for all values of $i$ and $j.$ \n\nGeometrically, when a matrix is transposed, the diagonal elements (those with $i=j$) remain the same while all other elements are reflected across the diagonal.  For example,\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6}\n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n\n      \\qquad  \\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & 1  \\\\\n        \t\t\\color{blue}{7} & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack.\n$$\n\nAlso,  transposing a matrix twice results in the original matrix. More precisely, for any matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( A^T\\right)^T = A.\n$$\n\nThis happens because, in the first transpose, we swap the rows of $A$ for the columns, and then in the second transpose, we swap them back again. For example,\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6}\n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n\n      \\quad  \\implies\\quad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & 1  \\\\\n        \t\t\\color{blue}{7} & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n\n      \\quad  \\implies\\quad\n\n\t\\left( {A}^T\\right)^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack =A.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any numbers $c_1,c_2$ and any $m \\times n$ matrices $A,B$ we have the following rule:\n\n$$\n(c_1 A \\pm c_2 B)^T = c_1 A^T \\pm c_2 B^T\n$$" "Remember that transposing a matrix swaps the rows and columns. So, if $B=A^T,$ then $b_{ij}=a_{ji}$ for all values of $i$ and $j.$ \n\nGeometrically, when a matrix is transposed, the diagonal elements (those with $i=j$) remain the same while all other elements are reflected across the diagonal.  For example,\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n      \\qquad  \\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & 1  \\\\\n        \t\t\\color{blue}{7} & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso,  transposing a matrix twice results in the original matrix. More precisely, for any matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( A^T\\right)^T = A.\n$$\n\nThis happens because, in the first transpose, we swap the rows of $A$ for the columns, and then in the second transpose, we swap them back again. For example,\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n      \\quad  \\implies\\quad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & 1  \\\\\n        \t\t\\color{blue}{7} & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n      \\quad  \\implies\\quad\n\n\t\\left( {A}^T\\right)^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix} =A.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any numbers $c_1,c_2$ and any $m \\times n$ matrices $A,B$ we have the following rule:\n\n$$\n(c_1 A \\pm c_2 B)^T = c_1 A^T \\pm c_2 B^T\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059576 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=1 \\\\ 0x+2y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\begin{matrix}\n\t\t1 & -2  \\\\\n\t\t0 & 2 \n\t\\end{matrix}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$" "First, we organize the system so that every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=1 \\\\ 0x+2y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t\t1 & -2  \\\\\n\t\t0 & 2 \n\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060394 :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y &=2 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y &=2 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059577 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-3z &=5 \\\\ -7x-y+z &=0 \\\\ 2x+y-2z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\begin{matrix}\n\t\t1 & 1 & -3  \\\\\n\t\t-7 & -1 & 1  \\\\\n                2 & 1 & -2 \n\t\\end{matrix}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$" "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-3z &=5 \\\\ -7x-y+z &=0 \\\\ 2x+y-2z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t\t1 & 1 & -3  \\\\\n\t\t-7 & -1 & 1  \\\\\n                2 & 1 & -2 \n\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060201 :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of  linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y &=5 \\\\ x+y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of  linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y &=5 \\\\ x+y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100688 :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/cd/cd3816d9938d4bd65d0eaa5abcb3db329aed3827219a8cea0186ced2d5759880.png)\nand\n![](images/b9/b9c909115c604eb1a96463bdd31a71e77caa0a11d0098a8144afd798b50ae236.png)" "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nand\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186100688 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 5 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 7\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/6d/6d470181fba760868835a72c5d5cfec3c90f9bf7bf7a9d86860dc8d981517652.png)" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 5 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 7\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186100689 :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/1c/1c38a037a66a5f86fd38a2b9ea6226420e455d5ef66fe776af7548fb6724841f.png)\nand\n![](images/ff/ffec9e0757e75cad35bbec65fb15d07eb580274c8beb4a9b3337d31cf964ec1c.png)" "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nand\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186100690 :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/52/528d30567626c47a051ea97c39cfe64a6bb4538d54b8a355d004457eca98d106.png)\nand\n![](images/e7/e7fe9e8a74f76dfec4fd92313fe84650f6006e3c54795fb06e3a255e4dbe8296.png)" "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nand\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186299816 :question/problem "Calculate $3A$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$3A=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "Calculate $3A$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$3A=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186299819 :question/problem "Calculate $-7A$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$-7A=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "Calculate $-7A$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$-7A=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186299820 :question/problem "Calculate $2C$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$2C=\\=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "Calculate $2C$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$2C=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186059550 :question/worked-solution "To express $kA,$ we multiply each entry of $A$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2k & 6k & 8k \\\\ 12k & 0 & -4k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kA$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t2k & 6k & 8k  \\\\\n        \t12k & 0 & -4k\n    \t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack\n=\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t1 & 3 & 4  \\\\\n        \t6 & 0 & -2\n    \t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-left elements, $2k$ and $1.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." "To express $kA,$ we multiply each entry of $A$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2k & 6k & 8k \\\\ 12k & 0 & -4k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kA$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t2k & 6k & 8k  \\\\\n        \t12k & 0 & -4k\n    \t\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t1 & 3 & 4  \\\\\n        \t6 & 0 & -2\n    \t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-left elements, $2k$ and $1.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."] [:db/cas 17592186299869 :question/problem "Calculate $4F-\\frac{1}{2}G$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$F=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nand